From 49734c4db2eb5fecd044168dc742a6a71858906e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:11:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] media: apple: vxd: add the command-buffer layer (R3b)

docs/periph/ds-vxd-cmdstream.md shows that the H.264 command stream is
an MSVDX register program.  Each slice is one chunk of command words in
a GART-mapped buffer plus one 0x78-byte packet describing that chunk.
The bitstream is not copied in: the 0xa command loads it from the
OUTPUT buffer's device address.  This patch adds the containers and
encodings that layout needs.  The H.264 values that go into them are
R3c.

Pool.  There are eight 64 KiB buffers, the size and count
DecodeCreateH264VLD allocates.  They come from one coherent block,
64 KiB aligned, and are GART-mapped during boot before the MTX starts,
so no mapping changes under a running core.  Each buffer moves through
FREE -> HOST (the CPU writes it) -> DEVICE (bound to the fence of its
last message) and back to FREE when that fence's 0xc0 arrives.  A fence
failed by a panic or a teardown wakes its waiter but leaves the buffer
quarantined, because the core may still be reading it; only a reclaim
after the firmware is stopped frees it.  An ownership walk runs on the
real pool at allocation: all eight are taken, a ninth is refused, and
alignment is checked.

Two buffers per submission.  The kext does not interleave packets with
commands.  At 0xfffffff0062967cc it computes the chunk byte count,
zero-fills 64 - (wptr & 63) bytes, and then calls CmdBuf::addCommand on
a different object (x23, not the command object in x19).  That object
has its own base, offset and capacity (0xfffffff0062982f8).
SubmitCommandBufferInternal then walks that object from its base,
checking both magics on every step.  The first command word, 0x90000000,
would fail that check.  So the register program is device memory, and
the {0x80000007, 0x78, 0xabcdefab} + payload packet list is a host-only
buffer that is turned into one mailbox message per packet.

Encodings, bit-exact with the emitters:
- 0x1 register runs.  Consecutive registers merge into one header
  (count += 1, 0xfffffff00629b068), but only inside one scoped group,
  because Apple's writer asserts exclusive use of the buffer.  Groups
  are explicit, and no other command may be emitted while one is open.
- 0x000f0000 single writes with the (flags & 0xf0) << 16 field.
- The all-zero 0x9 slice block.
- The 0xb descriptor.
- The 0xa load, with Apple's < 1 << 28 assert.
- The 0xf table transfer.
- The 0x6 terminator.
Chunks end with Apple's pad rule, a full 64 bytes when the buffer is
already aligned, and report the unpadded byte count (payload +0x0c).
Packets use addCommand's gate (offset + size + 0xc < capacity) and the
submit path's container walk.  Every emitter is bounds-checked into a
sticky error.

Messages.  apple_vxd_packet_msg() reproduces SubmitCommandBufferInternal
for kinds 1/3/4 (0x81/0x87/0x88):
- w1 = command words << 16 | flags.  The 0x81 flag (0x4004 last /
  0x2000 more) is OR-ed back into the packet, as the kext does.
- w2 = payload +0x10.
- w4 = payload +0x08 (0x87: & ~0x6000 | 0x4000).
- w5 = payload +0x24.
- w3 = (u32)(GART directory >> 8) with the low byte replaced by the
  hwContextID.

Correction to both studies: w3 is not the command buffer's address.
The kext loads it from +0x27a8 (lsr x8, x0, #8; str w8; strb w21 at
0xfffffff00628ae5c).  +0x27a8 is the 64 KiB-aligned GART directory
descriptor that ProcessorInitialise also programs into MTX_MMU_DIR_ADR
(0x1020) through 0xfffffff0062903d0.  The command buffer reaches the
firmware through payload +0x10, which is chunk_start + cmd->[0x18] [I].

V4L2 buffers.  buf_init/buf_cleanup now keep every OUTPUT and CAPTURE
buffer GART-mapped for its whole life, so the 0xa load can name the
bitstream and the render target and DPB registers can name the
surfaces.  Mapping only writes page-table memory, never a register, so
it is safe with the firmware down.  The decode STEP line logs both
device VAs.

A probe-time self-test runs the encodings, the padding, the packet list
and both message kinds on a scratch buffer, with no MMIO and no DMA.  A
failure keeps the firmware boot off.  device_run() still fails every job:
the generator is R3c.

Signed-off-by: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <maintainer@example.invalid>
